        </div>
        <h3 class="model-title">How they were built</h3>
        <p>Perry's houses had no plaster. The inside walls were half-inch insulating board, in sheets as large as 8 by 12 feet, laid out so that each room had no joint at all or a joint only above or below a window. Wallpaper went straight onto the board. Two men could finish the inside walls of a house in half a day to two days, and a whole house could go up in 30 days or less. Window frames arrived from the factory primed and weatherstripped, and the trim came cut to size. The outside walls were oyster-colored cement-asbestos shingles, the insulation was rock wool, and every house had a Bryant gas furnace and a Magic Chef gas range.</p>
          <figure class="figure figure-half">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open workers installing trim in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1938-construction.jpg" width="679" height="911" alt="workers installing trim" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>Carpenters fitting factory-cut trim on one of Perry's Westminster Ridge houses. <span class="credit">American Builder, October 1938.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>

        <h3 class="model-title">Which houses are Perry's?</h3>
        <p>County property records give a building year for every house in the neighborhood. They split cleanly into two groups. About 45 houses date from 1927 to 1937, Stafford's years. Another 44 date from 1938 to 1941, when Perry was building. The Perry-era houses sit exactly where the 1939 plot plan marks his "new development": the west end of Garretson Road, the west side of Chadwick Road, the south end of Gilbert Place, and the streets east of the pond.</p>
          <figure class="figure figure-wide">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open map of houses by building era in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/build-era-map.png" width="1260" height="1450" alt="map of houses by building era" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>Houses by the year they were built. Record years can be off by a year or two, and most houses have been enlarged since.</figcaption>
          </figure>

        <p>The houses recorded as built from 1938 to 1941 are:</p>
        <ul class="address-list">
          <li><strong>Chadwick Road:</strong> 2, 21, 25, 27, 36, 43, 47, 65, 66</li>
          <li><strong>Garretson Road:</strong> 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 14, 16, 20, 27, 33, 54, 56</li>
          <li><strong>Gilbert Place:</strong> 7, 11, 15</li>
          <li><strong>Southminster Drive:</strong> 2, 7, 8, 10, 11, 12, 16, 17, 18</li>
          <li><strong>Westminster Drive:</strong> 1, 3, 5, 9, 20</li>
          <li><strong>Lakeview Drive:</strong> 3, 5</li>
          <li><strong>Stafford Place:</strong> 5</li>
          <li><strong>Abbey Drive:</strong> 3</li>
        </ul>
        <p>To tell which design a house started as, look past the later additions to the original block. A Cape Cod with an attached one-car garage and two front dormers is probably a Greenfield. A smaller Cape with no attached garage and a porch or enclosed sunroom on the chimney end is probably a Lee. A two-story Colonial with an arched front portico is probably a Williamsburg. A stone-and-shingle house stepping down a slope, with the garage near the street, is probably a Westminster.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="lake" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="lake-title">
        <h2 id="lake-title">The neighbors buy the lake</h2>
        <p>The club ran the lake, but the original developers still owned it. When Perry's new families arrived in 1938, the club set out to buy it. In 1939 membership cost $2 a month. The dues paid for equipment, sand for the beach, and a lifeguard's salary, and admission fees went toward the purchase price.</p>
        <p>Two county aerial photographs show how the neighborhood filled in. The first, taken on December 19, 1940, shows Perry's new roofs around the pond, with open fields still south of it. By April 20, 1954, there are houses on almost every lot around the pond. Houses also line the streets on the hill to the east, which had been mostly woods in 1940.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1940 aerial photograph in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1940-aerial.jpg" width="900" height="990" alt="1940 aerial photograph showing the pond among neighborhood roads and houses" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>December 19, 1940. <span class="credit">Westchester County historical aerial collection.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1954 aerial photograph in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1954-aerial.jpg" width="900" height="990" alt="1954 aerial photograph showing the pond surrounded by houses" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>April 20, 1954, from roughly the same angle. <span class="credit">Westchester County historical aerial collection.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>The fishing was better than it looked. In September 1944 an 11-year-old named June Knapp was fishing in "a tiny pond at Westminster Ridge," water the neighbors considered fishless, when she hooked something too big to handle. A boy who ran to help couldn't land it either. A veteran angler, Carroll Ransford, came along and talked her through it, and she pulled in a 14-inch calico bass.</p>
        <p>By the 1950s the club's name was well known around town; one resident's <em>Who's Who in America</em> entry lists a seat on its board. By 1960 Lakenridge had a swim team that competed against other clubs in northern Westchester. It received federal tax-exempt status as a recreation club in 1973. Pasquale "Pat" Barile (1930–2020), a consulting engineer and White Plains civic leader, served as its president for many years. Today it is still run by neighbors. It hires lifeguards, runs a swim team and a kids' fishing derby, and opens each season with a lake cleanup.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-half">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the Lakenridge Club letter in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/lakenridge-letter.png" width="935" height="1210" alt="Lakenridge Club membership letter describing the lake season" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>A recent Lakenridge membership letter. Swimming, fishing, and the spring cleanup are still the center of the season.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="streets" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="streets-title">
        <h2 id="streets-title">The streets that were never built</h2>
        <p>Old maps of the neighborhood show more streets than exist today. The 1929–31 county atlas includes Bernard Place and Francis Place north of the pond, and a road called Stang Avenue running north from Colonial Place.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1929 to 1931 street atlas in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1930-reservoir.jpg" width="2600" height="2300" alt="1929 to 1931 county atlas showing Bernard Place, Francis Place, Colonial Place, Stang Avenue, and the reservoir" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1929–31. The planned streets around the reservoir. <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the present-day street map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/today-pond-map.png" width="768" height="768" alt="Present-day map showing Chadwick Road, Stafford Place, Gilbert Place, and Todds Pond" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>The same area today. Bernard Place and Francis Place are gone.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Most of the old names are still here under new ones. Colonial Place was built around 1908. In 1934 it was briefly called Chadwick Place, then renamed Stafford Place that September. The road now called Chadwick Road started as Colonial Terrace. Its northern extension, added around 1927, was first named Stang Avenue. In 1934 the whole road became Chadwick Road.</p>
        <p>Bernard Place and Francis Place were never built. They stayed on the city map as "paper streets," and Chadwick Road stops short of where Bernard Place would have been. In 2012 the city moved to buy the woods between Chadwick and Bernard as a wildlife corridor and connected them to city-owned land along Francis Place. The streets that were never built became open space instead.</p>
        <p class="source-line">Street history from <a href="https://wpstreets.weebly.com/home/stafford-place" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Streets of White Plains ↗</a> and the <a href="https://dailyvoice.com/article/white-plains-to-buy-wildlife-corridor-for-130k/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daily Voice ↗</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="people" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="people-title">
        <h2 id="people-title">Who grew up here</h2>
        <p>Most of the people who lived on the ridge left little public record, but a few did. Stephen Holden III was born in White Plains and raised in Westminster Ridge. He became a local lawyer and civic leader who worked for historic preservation in the city. In 2009 the New York State Senate passed a resolution honoring his work, and it mentions the neighborhood by name.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-half">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the resolution honoring Stephen Holden III in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/holden-resolution.jpg" width="910" height="690" alt="2009 legislative resolution honoring Stephen Holden III" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The 2009 Senate resolution for Stephen Holden III. <span class="credit">New York State Senate.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>If you have old photographs of the pond, the beach, or the houses going up, or stories from people who remember the early lake club, we would like to hear from you. Much of this history is still in attics and family albums.</p>
      </section>

